{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310749,"nonce":"z7JqFBKSA2iO0rtK8rhvyTHJ4Z0=","title":"rayzor's wasm linker (compiler/src/codegen/wasm_linker.rs) re-encodes every runtime-wasm Op-\u003eInstruction in a giant match whose catch-all ...","message":"**Root cause (2026-06-15).** `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_linker.rs` converts the\nruntime `.wasm` instruction-by-instruction (`Op` from wasmparser 0.245 →\n`Instruction` for wasm-encoder 0.245) to merge it with the user wasm. The match\nhad a catch-all `_ =\u003e Instruction::Unreachable` (was ~line 2164). Any opcode\nwithout an explicit arm was SILENTLY rewritten to `unreachable`. When the Q4_K\nguest kernel `vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K_simd128` started emitting\n`i32x4.relaxed_dot_i8x16_i7x16_add_s`, the linker turned it into `unreachable`,\nso calling it (via `rayzor_qtensor_matmul_f32` ← `QTensor_fusedQkvMatmul`)\ntrapped on the in-guest path (`RAYZOR_WASM_NO_HOST_MATMUL=1`). The DEFAULT path\nwas fine because it routes the matmul to the native host pool, bypassing the\nguest kernel.\n\n**FIX.** Added all 20 relaxed-simd ops (1:1 names in wasmparser/wasm-encoder\n0.245, all no-immediate) before the catch-all, and changed the catch-all to\n`other =\u003e { eprintln!(\"[wasm-linker] WARNING: unhandled operator {other:?} ...\");\nInstruction::Unreachable }` so the next gap is visible, not silent.\n\n**Result — rayzor wasm Q4/Q6 matmul now uses relaxed_dot → NEON SDOT.** With the\nlocal patched cranelift (sdot lowering, [[perf_wasm_v8_vs_wasmtime_2x]]) + the\nkernel switch (q4_k_m.rs + q6_k.rs `dot16` → `i32x4_relaxed_dot_i8x16_i7x16_add`,\noperand order activation_i8 / weight_i7, accumulate into c) + this linker fix:\n- in-guest wasm decode (Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, single-thread, NO_HOST_MATMUL):\n  **3.01 → 4.70 tok/s = 1.56x**, output bit-identical (Q4 nibbles 0..15 and Q6\n  weights 0..63 are valid i7, so relaxed_dot is exact — no i16 saturation).\n  Q4 alone = 1.32x; +Q6 = 1.56x. Runtime now `relaxed_dot=24, dot_i16x8=0`.\n- NOTE the Q6 workflow claim \"relaxed_dot unsafe for Q6_K (signed -32..31)\" was\n  WRONG: the kernel reconstructs weights as 0..63 unsigned and folds the -32 bias\n  via bsums, so they ARE valid i7.\n\n**Two methodology lessons:**\n1. Don't call an in-guest wasm trap \"pre-existing\" from reasoning alone. A 1-line\n   control test (revert the kernel → dot_i16x8, rebuild runtime-wasm/build.sh,\n   re-run in-guest) instantly showed dot_i16x8 decodes fine while relaxed_dot\n   traps → it was OUR change, not pre-existing.\n2. rayzor uses the rebuilt `runtime-wasm/target/wasm32-wasip1-threads/release/\n   rayzor_runtime_wasm.wasm` (rm -rf .rayzor forces a fresh link), so editing the\n   kernel + `runtime-wasm/build.sh` is enough to test guest-kernel changes — no\n   rayzor rebuild needed (only a compiler rebuild for the linker fix itself).\n\n**REMAINING GAP (open):** the in-guest path is single-threaded (4.70 tok/s). The\nDEFAULT path uses the native host worker pool (fast, but native, bypasses the\nguest kernel). The BROWSER has no host pool → in-guest only → needs in-wasm Web\nWorker / wasi-thread threading to be performant. That is the next big wasm-perf\nlever, separate from this dot work.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_linker_silent_unreachable` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}